  "sourceAndTextContext": {
    "independentSourcePixelObservationsScopedToLabel": [
      "The source has three rows in two columns. The upper-right subfigure is the A-to-A-prime focused case with a reduced opening, paired with the upper-left A case.",
      "Its source panel identifier is below that subfigure. Moving the English d identifier above the corresponding modern subfigure preserves attribution, rather than asserting that its original pixel position was above.",
      "In the right-column source subfigures, the stop bars are on the image side, immediately to the right of the lens. The unchanged English prose says before the objective; the Chinese prose also says before the lens. This is an unresolved scientific/source-text disagreement."
    ],
    "authoritativeTextLocation": "source.authoritativeText in the hash-pinned candidate/spec.json; relevant bilingual passages only. No DB or live lesson was accessed.",
    "relevantTextEntries": [
      {
        "jsonPointer": "/source/authoritativeText/5",
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s2-n78:zh",
        "text": "假设相机对准某一距离上的发光点 $A$ 调焦；\n在薄透镜、近轴几何光学的理想模型中，它的像落在胶片上，是一个点（图 182a）。\n离镜头较近的点 $B$（图 182b）和离镜头较远的点 $C$（图 182c），在同一个胶片平面上则形成散焦的小圆斑。\n\n在镜头前加上光圈（图 182d、e、f）后，有效孔径越小，散焦圆斑的直径就越小（图 182e、f）。\n因此，缩小光圈可以使一定范围内、离相机不同距离的物体都显得足够清晰，这就是景深增加。\n“足够清晰”不表示这些物体全都精确成像于同一个平面，也不表示散焦圆斑都变成了数学上的点。\n\n这一结论适用于几何光学散焦占主导的范围；\n光圈很小时，衍射也会使像变模糊。\n真实镜头还存在像差，所以理想模型中的像点不表示任意角度、任意有限孔径的光线都严格交于一点。"
      },
      {
        "jsonPointer": "/source/authoritativeText/14",
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s2-n78:en",
        "text": "Suppose the camera is focused on a luminous point $A$ at a particular distance.\nIn the ideal thin-lens, paraxial geometrical-optics model, its film image is a point (Figure 182a).\n\nPoint $B$ (Figure 182b) is nearer, and point $C$ (Figure 182c) is farther.\nBoth form small defocus circles on that same film plane.\n\nAn aperture is placed before the objective (Figure 182d, e, f).\nA smaller effective opening gives smaller defocus circles (Figure 182e, f).\n\nStopping down makes a range of object distances look sufficiently sharp: depth of field increases.\nSufficient sharpness does not put all objects in exact focus on the same plane.\n\nSufficient sharpness need not make every defocus circle a mathematical point.\nThis holds where geometrical defocus dominates.\n\nAt very small apertures, diffraction also blurs the image.\nReal lenses also have aberrations.\nThe ideal image point does not imply exact concurrence for arbitrary ray angles or finite apertures."
      }
    ],
    "meaningUsedForLabelAttribution": "The relevant unchanged bilingual prose distinguishes the focused A case (a/d) from nearer B (b/e) and farther C (c/f). It also contains the unresolved before-objective aperture statement.",
